hey kids do you like hearthstone how  about the Elder Scrolls well the Elder  Scrolls legends might just be the game  for you I've spent a good amount of time  with the beta and while they move around  some of the names anyone familiar with  bar stone will instantly understand the  game's mechanics and layout online  versus arena mode microtransactions it's  all here however there are some  differences that make the game stand out  when you start up for the first time you  pick one of the ten races from the  series which race you will pick  influences the type of cards you will  collect and earn allowing you to more  easily gain cards for the deck and play  style you want to build you can of  course change or race at any time  legends features a full story mode which  functions as the game's tutorial in fact  you have to play it to certain points in  order to unlock access to the game's  other modes but it's worth going through  the whole thing to unlock cards and  starter decks the story even attempts to  bring in some of that elder scrolls  flavor by having moral choices these  choices don't change the story in any  way but rather have you choose between  two cards in game one of the big  differences you'll notice right away is  lanes creatures in one lane can't affect  the other adding an extra layer of  strategy in your card placements lanes  also have effects on them such as  protecting creatures for the first term  in there and play or randomly blowing a  card to the other Lane there is also the  ruins system five of your cards are  randomly placed inside ruins every loss  of five health breaks the ruin and you  get that card in your hand if the card  is labeled prophecy you get to play it  for free it's a nice way to give the  losing player potential boost so the  match doesn't become one sided for deck  building you can build your deck around  two of the different card types decks  have a minimum of 50 cards than a max of  70 you can also have three of the same  card in a deck instead of two like  hearthstone another interesting little  mechanic is that after lots of use some  cards will get upgrades allowing you to  choose between two different superior  versions it's an interesting idea that  your cards will get better the more you  play but I wonder how this will affect  the balance of the game  of course this is an Elder Scrolls game  and features plenty of fun little knobs  to the series such as the arena  announcer from oblivion the adoring fan  and yes that one the Elder Scrolls  Legends is a well made game and I had a  lot of fun with it but it's also a  familiar one I'll be curious to see how  much staying power and fan bases has  when the game is fully released later  this year on PC and iOS if you want to  try the game out for yourself before  that though there is a beta that you can  go sign up for and if you want to see  more coverage of this game stay tuned  gamespot  home early
 

